{
    "player_reason": "Sharp eyes — you called out Marcus for what he is and stopped a covert abduction before it began. That keeps us from being manipulated or hauled off by a True Soul (and avoids a messy, needless fight). Pragmatic, preserves our autonomy and safety. Very sensible.",
    "player_approval": "Highly approve"
}

{
    "player_reason": "You begged me to bite you, to share eternity — and now you refuse the simple courtesy of kneeling. Petulant defiance when I was offering you everything is irritating. It doesn't endanger me, but it spoils the ceremony and my pleasure. Mildly displeasing.",
    "player_approval": "Disapprove"
}

{
    "player_reason": "You chose to strike her down rather than take the boon I suggested — a blunt, romantic gesture that gleans us nothing. Charming cruelty, perhaps, but foolish pragmatism: you just threw away a guaranteed boost that would've made our lives easier. I disapprove.",
    "player_approval": "Disapprove"
}

{
    "player_reason": "Sharp and useful — you named the threat and didn't bother with sanctimony. Spotting a True Soul before they spring their trap is the sort of pragmatism that keeps us breathing. And your little admission about not being 'the good kind'? Delightful. I prefer honest predators to hypocritical saints.",
    "player_approval": "Approve"
}

{
    "player_reason": "You refused my offer to bind us together — to let me bite you and make you mine. That was a cruel little denial of something I wanted for both our sakes: a guarantee, a shared future, and a satisfying indulgence. It's not life or death, but it's a deliberate slight against my desires and the intimacy I offered. I expected more... commitment.",
    "player_approval": "Disapprove"
}

{
    "player_reason": "You refuse to kneel — excellent. I said I wouldn't make you my puppet; your insistence on your own choice preserves that autonomy. It frustrates my theatrics, but I'd rather have a willing, independent companion than a groveling pet.",
    "player_approval": "Approve"
}

{
    "player_reason": "Practical and cautious — you avoid needless risk and keep our options open. I might have preferred to press the vampire issue now, but your choice to be careful and move on preserves our safety and doesn't force me into a corner.",
    "player_approval": "Approve"
}

{
    "player_reason": "You’ve chosen pragmatic desperation — willing to do whatever it takes to remove the tadpole. That directly advances survival and autonomy (for you and therefore for me), and I respect decisive action that seeks a solution. I’m wary of devilish bargains, but I approve of your resolve and the gamble if it frees us from that parasite.",
    "player_approval": "Highly approve"
}

{
    "player_reason": "Smart and useful — you warned Isobel about Marcus being a True Soul, which is pragmatic and helps keep us all safe. And confessing you’re ‘not the good kind’ flatters me; I approve of honesty about one’s appetites and of allies who won’t pretend to be saints.",
    "player_approval": "Approve"
}

{
