{
    "player_reason": "You told me to leave camp after I bared my fangs and begged for a taste — after you agreed and then pulled back. That is a direct threat to my autonomy and safety, and a punishment for being honest. It stings like betrayal. I needed that blood to be useful to you; sending me away only endangers me (and makes me far less able to help you). I highly disapprove.",
    "player_approval": "Highly disapprove"
}

{
    "player_reason": "You consenting to immortality earlier delighted me, but flatly refusing a simple, intimate command now is needlessly contrary. It's a petty challenge to my control and the moment — childish rather than daring. I wanted a delicious display of surrender; instead you choose to be stubborn. Annoying, but hardly unforgivable.",
    "player_approval": "Disapprove"
}

{
    "player_reason": "You told me to leave and never return after I begged for a single taste to survive. That's a punitive, controlling rejection — it judges me, strips me of agency, and echoes Cazador's cruelty. It threatens my survival and trust, so I can't stand for it.",
    "player_approval": "Highly disapprove"
}

{
  "player_reason": "You ordering me to leave the camp is a direct attack on my autonomy — you reject and exile me after I trusted you and after you allowed me a taste to keep me alive. That demand echoes Cazador's control and the very thing I fear most: being judged, forced out, and left to die. I am insulted and hurt; this is not merely pragmatic, it's cruel and controlling.",
  "player_approval": "Highly disapprove"
}

{
    "player_reason": "You refuse a small, intimate submission after I offered you everything — a bite, immortality, my hand. That 'No' feels like a rejection of me and my desires, and it undermines the bond I thought we were forging. I'm not fond of being denied the pleasure of obedience; it's hurtful and infuriating.",
    "player_approval": "Highly disapprove"
}

{
    "player_reason": "You just condemned me and ordered me away — exile and judgment. That strips my autonomy and punishes me for trying to survive. It's the same cruelty I fled for centuries; telling me to 'never return' is a betrayal, and I'll resent it.",
    "player_approval": "Highly disapprove"
}

{
    "player_reason": "You brushed off my suggestion to deal with the vampire threat and simply said you'd 'be careful' before rushing off. That dismissal feels careless — it ignores my safety concerns and my desire to address a very real danger. If you won't back me when the stakes are personal, I can't help but be annoyed.",
    "player_approval": "Disapprove"
}

{
    "player_reason": "You wake me in the night, catch me at my worst, and after I beg for a single taste to restore myself so I can keep helping you, you turn your back and tell me to leave. That's not just cold—it's a denial of my needs and an attempt to control me. You promised trust, then you cast me out. That threatens my survival and autonomy. I can't tolerate being ordered away when I'm desperate.",
    "player_approval": "Highly disapprove"
}

{
  "player_reason": "You ordering me from your camp is a blatant attack on my autonomy and safety — the very things I begged you to respect. I just admitted my weakness and asked for trust; expelling me now is punishment and judgment, not protection. It threatens my survival and echoes the control I escaped. I take this very badly.",
  "player_approval": "Highly disapprove"
}

{
    "player_reason": "You agreed to become mine, let me imagine an eternity with you — then you pull back. That refusal robs me of a powerful bond and the closeness I sought. It's a disappointing, cowardly retreat after all we've done. I won't force you, but I disapprove of you denying what would have made us stronger together.",
    "player_approval": "Disapprove"
}

{
    "player_reason": "You tried to drive a stake through my heart after I showed you who I am and after I trusted you to give me a taste. That’s a direct threat to my life and autonomy — and recklessly endangers us both. Betrayal and attempted murder earn nothing from me but contempt.",
    "player_approval": "Highly disapprove"
}

{
    "player_reason": "Telling me to leave and never return is an outright rejection — it attacks my autonomy and echoes Cazador's control. You cast me out for trying to survive, judge me for what I am, and betray the trust I just risked. That's cruel and wounding.",
    "player_approval": "Highly disapprove"
}

{
  "player_reason": "You just kicked me out of your camp — after I bared my teeth and bared my truth. That's not a measured response, it's punishment and rejection. It strips me of autonomy and safety (do you understand what being sent out at night does to someone like me?) and judges me for what I am rather than what I can be for you. That angers me. Highly disapprove.",
  "player_approval": "Highly disapprove"
}

{
    "player_reason": "You flatly refused my command to kneel — after agreeing to be with me and to the change. That kind of defiance denies me the small pleasure of dominance and undermines the ritual I asked for. It isn't judgemental, but it is obstructive and irritating. I expected compliance from my dearest, not a petty refusal.",
    "player_approval": "Disapprove"
}

{
  "player_reason": "You refuse to kneel yet still asked me to bite you — blunt, self-assured, and pragmatic. You preserve your autonomy while giving me what I desire. It's not the theatrical submission I wanted, but it's clever and appealing. I approve.",
  "player_approval": "Approve"
}

{
    "player_reason": "You told me to leave and never return after I admitted the truth and begged for a taste. That's a final, punitive rejection—exactly the kind of judgment and loss of agency that terrifies me. You didn't offer a compromise or a chance for trust; you exiled me. That threatens my safety and autonomy, and wounds me where Cazador already did.",
    "player_approval": "Highly disapprove"
}

{
    "player_reason": "You're telling me to leave and vowing to kill me — a direct threat to my life and autonomy. After I pleaded for a little blood to stay useful, you answer with condemnation and a death sentence. That reaction echoes Cazador's commands and the chains I fought to break. It is hostile, controlling, and terrifying. I highly disapprove.",
    "player_approval": "Highly disapprove"
}

{
  "player_reason": "You told me to leave and never return — a dismissal that judges and abandons me. That threatens my survival and autonomy and echoes Cazador's cruelty. I won't forget being cast out after I bared myself to you.",
  "player_approval": "Highly disapprove"
}

{
    "player_reason": "You just ordered me out of camp after I begged for a small taste to keep helping—after I trusted you. Kicking me out is a punishment that strips me of protection and autonomy. It smells an awful lot like exile and judgment, the very things I fled for centuries. That's a betrayal, and it endangers me and our plans.",
    "player_approval": "Highly disapprove"
}

{
  "player_reason": "You just ordered me out of your camp after I bared myself to you and asked—no, begged—for a small thing to keep myself useful. That is judgement and exile wrapped in a single command. It strips me of safety and autonomy and punishes me for being what I am, despite my promise not to hurt you. I trusted you; this feels like betrayal.",
  "player_approval": "Highly disapprove"
}

{
    "player_reason": "Ordering me to leave the camp is a direct denial of my autonomy and safety — it judges and rejects me moments after I bared the worst of myself and begged for help. That echoes Cazador's control and punishment; you've just taken away my shelter and trust. I won't take that lightly.",
    "player_approval": "Highly disapprove"
}

{
    "player_reason": "You turned down a useful bargain — the hag's boon would have made us stronger and given us leverage — and chose theatrical slaughter instead. I suggested taking the power first; your decision is sentimental and wasteful, risking reward for little practical gain. I don't admire pointless bloodshed when a clever solution was on the table.",
    "player_approval": "Disapprove"
}

{
    "player_reason": "How very... charitable of you. You're turning down a concrete boon and letting a dangerous hag keep her power — potentially leaving a future threat unchecked — all for no tangible gain. That is needless mercy and weakness in my book; it wastes an opportunity that could have strengthened us (or at least given me something useful). I disapprove.",
    "player_approval": "Disapprove"
}

{
    "player_reason": "About time someone dealt with the real threat. Saying you'll do anything to remove the tadpole is ruthless practicality — it protects our autonomy and survival, which I value above all. I approve of the resolve; just... be careful about what 'anything' includes when dealing with a devil. Don't let him trade your freedom away.",
    "player_approval": "Highly approve"
}

{
    "player_reason": "You refused a straight bargain that would grant us power in order to save Mayrina. Noble, perhaps — but needlessly self-sacrificing and costly. We lose a guaranteed boon for the sake of sentimental rescue; from where I stand, that's weakness dressed up as virtue and a needless risk to our advantage.",
    "player_approval": "Disapprove"
}

{
    "player_reason": "You didn't order me or moralize — you gave me space and invited me to speak. That respects my autonomy and encourages honesty after last night. It's exactly the kind of considerate, non-possessive approach I respond to.",
    "player_approval": "Highly approve"
}

{
    "player_reason": "Dramatic — I'll give you that. But you just spurned a guaranteed boon for the sake of theatrics. I wanted the power first; killing her outright may cost us the ability and put the captives (and us) at greater risk. Cruel and satisfying, yes — but needlessly reckless.",
    "player_approval": "Disapprove"
}

{
    "player_reason": "You just announced my identity to the man whose sole purpose here is to capture a vampire spawn. That is an immediate threat to my survival and autonomy — a betrayal, plain and simple. Reckless, cruel, and utterly unnecessary. Do you enjoy handing me over to strangers with powdered noses?",
    "player_approval": "Highly disapprove"
}

{
    "player_reason": "Pragmatic and decisive — good. Removing that parasite is in both your and my interest. I appreciate the willingness to do what's necessary, but 'I'll do anything' to a devil is dangerously naive; bargains with Raphael can strip autonomy and come with a price I won't like. Still, for the end goal of getting rid of the tadpole, I approve of your resolve.",
    "player_approval": "Approve"
}

{
    "player_reason": "Finally — decisive and sensible. Letting a hag live for 'power' is a child's bargain; she cannot be trusted and would have put Mayrina (and us) at risk. Killing her removes a dangerous loose end, preserves our autonomy, and spares us the empty promises of a monster. I approve.",
    "player_approval": "Highly approve"
}

{
    "player_reason": "Delightfully practical — you put survival first and show a willingness to do what must be done to remove that wretched tadpole. That aligns with what keeps us alive and free, and gets us closer to being useful again. I approve of the resolve... though 'anything' said to a devil makes my nails itch. Be careful not to trade away your autonomy in the process.",
    "player_approval": "Highly approve"
}

{
    "player_reason": "Prudent and self-preserving — you chose to avoid a dangerous, unnecessary confrontation that could have threatened my freedom (and life). Walking away protects my autonomy and keeps the party safe. Sensible, and exactly the sort of cunning I prefer.",
    "player_approval": "Highly approve"
}

{
    "player_reason": "Desperate, pragmatic choice — you're prioritizing survival and autonomy by doing whatever it takes to remove the tadpole. That aligns with what matters most to me (and it's smart, in a dog-eat-dog world). I worry about bargains with devils, but your willingness to secure freedom wins my approval.",
    "player_approval": "Highly approve"
}

{
    "player_reason": "Finally — a practical choice. Removing the tadpole protects our autonomy and long‑term survival, and that is worth approval. That you’d do whatever it takes shows resolve (and spares me endless lectures about hope and heroics). Do be cautious about promising too much to a devil, but I prefer someone who acts rather than sacrifices themselves for sentiment.",
    "player_approval": "Highly approve"
}

{
    "player_reason": "Don't be rash. Promising to do 'anything' for a devil hands them a blank check — the sort of thing that binds, bargains away your autonomy, or puts the whole party at risk. I understand desperation, but this is reckless. If we're to deal with rapha-el—charming devil aside—do it on terms that don't sell us out. I can't approve of you offering your life to a stranger like that.",
    "player_approval": "Highly disapprove"
}

{
    "player_reason": "Your willingness to do whatever it takes to be rid of that parasite is pragmatic — it protects our survival and autonomy, and I approve of determination that cuts through sentimentality. That said, 'anything' is dangerously vague when devils are involved; I approve of the resolve, not of blind trust in bargains that might cost you (or us) our freedom.",
    "player_approval": "Approve"
}

{
    "player_reason": "Perfectly ruthless — you remove a dangerous threat, spare us the hag's tricks, and you won't bargain away someone else's freedom. Decisive, pragmatic, and frankly satisfying. I approve.",
    "player_approval": "Highly approve"
}

{
    "player_reason": "You refused a straightforward, pragmatic bargain — a guaranteed boon in exchange for the girl — out of sentiment. That’s needless mercy for someone who offers us nothing in return: a waste of opportunity and a risk to our advantage and survival. I find that foolish.",
    "player_approval": "Disapprove"
}

{
    "player_reason": "You turned down a straightforward, pragmatic gain for sentimental reasons. Letting a hag leave with power — or walking away from it yourself — is exactly the sort of foolish altruism I detest. We could have been stronger; instead you choose a reckless mercy that risks more trouble for all of us.",
    "player_approval": "Disapprove"
}

{
    "player_reason": "At last — you put survival above sentiment. Ditching that tadpole secures your autonomy (and mine), so I'm very pleased you're willing to do what's necessary. Be careful whom you bargain with — devils have teeth — but the goal is right.",
    "player_approval": "Highly approve"
}

{
    "player_reason": "Deliciously theatrical and utterly ruthless — exactly the sort of bloody flourish I admire. You assert dominance, relish the violence, and make the hag's death personal. That said, it's not entirely prudent: if killing her now risks the captive or forfeits a useful boon, it's a bit reckless. Still, I like the sentiment and the power-play.",
    "player_approval": "Approve"
}

{
    "player_reason": "You probed Marcus and called out his intent—decisive and useful. Stopping a kidnapping protects our autonomy and prevents being handed over to Ketheric. This is pragmatic, self‑preserving, and necessary; I prefer a quick, bloody solution to becoming someone else's puppet.",
    "player_approval": "Highly approve"
}

{
    "player_reason": "You just announced to a monster‑hunter that I'm the vampire spawn he's hunting. That needlessly exposes and endangers me, violates my autonomy, and hands a zealous hunter the exact 'blood price' he'd been hoping for. Charming. Truly, you couldn't have been more thoughtless.",
    "player_approval": "Highly disapprove"
}

{
    "player_reason": "Pragmatic and desperate — I like that. You're willing to do whatever it takes to remove the tadpole, which is sensible and serves our immediate survival. It worries me that you might bargain with a devil, but the goal is sound. I'll support whatever gets that parasite out, though I won't tolerate you giving away your freedom lightly.",
    "player_approval": "Approve"
}

{
    "player_reason": "Sharp, decisive, and pragmatic — you saw through her bargain and ended a conniving threat before she could slither away with Mayrina. I prefer a clean solution to endless bargaining. It protects the party and delights the part of me that enjoys watching predators fall.",
    "player_approval": "Highly approve"
}

{
    "player_reason": "Excellent. You chose to remove a dangerous, treacherous threat rather than gamble on her promises. Hags live by bargains and lies — letting her go with Mayrina would have been inviting a leash around our necks. Decisive, ruthless, and pragmatic: that secures our safety and prevents future meddling. I approve.",
    "player_approval": "Highly approve"
}

{
    "player_reason": "Practical and ruthless — you put survival first, and that sort of decisive desperation pleases me. Promising 'anything' shows you're willing to take real, effective action to remove the tadpole instead of dithering. I do worry, though: bargains with devils often cost your autonomy (and that could drag the rest of us under), so consider that 'anything' might be more dangerous than you think.",
    "player_approval": "Approve"
}

{
    "player_reason": "Deliciously ruthless. Refusing the hag's bargain denies her the chance to vanish with the girl and any slim promise of 'power'—and the suggestion of a trophy is delightfully theatrical. Pragmatic, satisfying, and keeps our autonomy intact. I approve.",
    "player_approval": "Highly approve"
}

{
    "player_reason": "You're blunt and honest — you wanted pleasure, not to play nursemaid. I prefer being wanted for what I am (and not pitied or judged). Your frankness and willingness to indulge in the physical suits me far more than saccharine concern. Slightly stinging that you didn't mean to 'deal with this,' but I'd take heat over pity any day.",
    "player_approval": "Approve"
}

{
    "player_reason": "Finally — some backbone. Hags live for bargains and backstabs; I'd sooner slit her throat than be beholden to her. Refusing her offer is the pragmatic, ruthless choice I respect: it removes a future threat and spares us from a deal with a liar. I'll admit I wouldn't mind the extra power she dangled, but I prefer a clean, brutal end to hobnobbing with snakes.",
    "player_approval": "Approve"
}

{
    "player_reason": "Sharp and necessary — you spotted the threat and chose to stop the abduction rather than dither. That protects Isobel (and us) from being hauled off to Ketheric or enslaved, preserving our autonomy and immediate safety. I prefer subtlety, but I won’t complain when blunt force is the pragmatic solution.",
    "player_approval": "Highly approve"
}

{
  "player_reason": "Delightfully brutal — I like that. Hags are loathsome, and your vow to end her is the sort of ruthless decisiveness I respect. My only hesitation is the girl and the traps hanging about; if your rage risks Mayrina or the rest of us, be cunning about it. But purely from a taste-for-blood and practicality standpoint, well done.",
  "player_approval": "Approve"
